t me simplify and just focus on the main changes.\n\nActually, let me step back. The most impactful and highest-priority change is the naming one. Let me just do that one refactoring well.\n\n**Naming Evaluation:**\n- Current name: `survives`\n- Function purpose: \"determines whether a cell (alive or dead) will be alive in the next generation\"\n- Question: Does \"survives\" clearly reveal this intent?\n- Assessment: No - \"survives\" implies the cell is currently alive and continues living. But this function also returns true for dead cells with 3 neighbors (reproduction). The name is misleading.\n- Recommendation: Rename to `willBeAlive`\n\nThis is a Rule 2 (Reveals Intent) improvement, which is the highest priority after tests passing.\n\nLet me also check: are there other naming issues?\n\n- `cellKey` - Creates a string key from a cell. Clear. ✓\n- `keyToCell` - Converts a string key back to a cell. Clear. ✓\n- `countNeighbors` - Counts live neighbors of a cell. Clear. ✓\n- `nextGeneration` - Computes the next generation. Clear. ✓\n- `liveSet` - Set of live cell keys. Clear. ✓\n- `candidates` - All cells that could potentially be alive. Reasonable. Could be renamed to `candidateKeys` to be more specific since it contains keys, not cells. That's a small improvement.\n\nOK, let me do the refactoring:\n\n1. Rename `survives` → `willBeAlive` (Rule 2)\n\nAnd let me also consider removing the unnecessary empty check:\n\n2. Remove `if (cells.length === 0) return [];` - This is handled naturally by the algorithm (Rule 4)\n\nLet me also consider: can I eliminate the `keyToCell` function? It's only used in one place and creates overhead. But it helps readability, so keeping it is fine per Rule 2.\n\nLet me also look at whether I can eliminate `cellKey` or `keyToCell` by using a different data structure. Actually, what if I use a `Map<string, Cell>` for `candidates`? Then I wouldn't need `keyToCell` at all.\n\nHmm, but that would add complexity. The current approach is simpler.\n\nLet me just do the two changes:\n\n1. Rename `survives` → `willBeAlive`\n2. Remove unnecessary empty check\n\nWait, I should do them one at a time according to the process. But actually, the rename is safe and the empty check removal is also safe, and they're independent. Let me do them both, run tests, and verify.\n\nActually, let me think more carefully. Is there a more substantial refactoring I could do?\n\nLooking at the code again:\n\n```typescript\nfor (const cell of cells) {\n    for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n      candidates.add(cellKey([cell[0] + dx, cell[1] + dy]));\n    }\n    candidates.add(cellKey(cell));\n  }\n```\n\nThis iterates over all live cells and adds them and their neighbors as candidates. The inner loop adds neighbors, then the outer adds the cell itself. This could be simplified by using `flatMap` or similar, but probably at the cost of readability.\n\nWhat about the `countNeighbors` function? It takes a Cell and a Set<string>. The lookup pattern `liveSet.has(cellKey([...]))` creates a temporary array on every call. For performance, we could compute the key string directly without the intermediate array. But this would duplicate the key format, violating DRY.\n\nAlternatively, we could have `countNeighbors` take a key string directly, and compute neighbor keys by string manipulation. But that's ugly.\n\nOr we could change `cellKey` to take two numbers directly instead of a Cell tuple:\n\n```typescript\nfunction cellKey(x: number, y: number): string {\n  return `${x},${y}`;\n}\n```\n\nThis avoids creating temporary Cell arrays in `countNeighbors`. Let me see all the call sites:\n\n1. `const liveSet = new Set(cells.map(cellKey));` - Currently `cells.map(cellKey)` passes each Cell to cellKey. With the new signature, this would be `cells.map(([x, y]) => cellKey(x, y))`.\n\n2. `candidates.add(cellKey([cell[0] + dx, cell[1] + dy]));` → `candidates.add(cellKey(cell[0] + dx, cell[1] + dy));`\n\n3. `candidates.add(cellKey(cell)