r (const cell of cells) {\n    for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n      candidates.add(cellKey([cell[0] + dx, cell[1] + dy]));\n    }\n    candidates.add(cellKey(cell));\n  }\n\n  const result: Cell[] = [];\n  for (const key of candidates) {\n    const cell = keyToCell(key);\n    const neighbors = countNeighbors(cell, liveSet);\n    if (willLive(liveSet.has(key), neighbors)) {\n      result.push(cell);\n    }\n  }\n\n  result.sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0] || a[1] - b[1]);\n\n  return result;\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784967060212},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Step 5: Calculate New APP Mass\n\nMass is unchanged (renaming doesn't alter code elements): **141 → 141 (delta 0)**. This is expected — the improvement is in Rule 2 (Reveals Intent), which trumps APP.\n\n## Step 6: Document Decision\n\n**Refactoring Applied**:\n- **Naming**: Renamed `survives` → `willLive` (better reveals intent)\n- **Mass**: 141 → 141 (delta 0 — renaming preserves mass)\n- **Rule 2**: The function determines whether a cell will be alive in the next generation, covering both *survival* (live cell with 2–3 neighbors) and *reproduction* (dead cell with exactly 3 neighbors). The old name `survives` only described the survival case, making the reproduction case conceptually incorrect. `willLive` accurately captures both cases.\n\n**Other rules evaluated, no action needed**:\n- **Rule 3 (DRY)**: No meaningful duplication found. `cellKey` is used consistently without redundancy.\n- **Rule 4 (Fewest Elements)**: The `if (cells.length === 0) return []` guard is technically redundant but reveals intent (Rule 2 > Rule 4), so it stays. All other elements serve clear purposes.\n\n```\nRefactoring Complete:\n**Refactoring**: Renamed `survives` → `willLive` to accurately reveal intent (survival + reproduction)\n**Mass Change**: 141 → 141 (delta 0)  \n**Tests**: All 11 passing\n```"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"nebius/zai-org/glm-5.1"